XR diorama: each eye's scene scissored to the table, and each frame's own head pose and mask

For WakerQuest's diorama (mode maqueta, pc_vr_diorama.cpp) and window (patches 0025, 0026, 0030, 0033):
- The table's scissor: in the diorama's frames (the mask's fade[3] 0, the box, or 1, the bubble) the
  scene's passes in each eye get a scissor, as the window's (patch 0033), to the bounds of what the mask
  can show: the box (x and z within +-1, from the cut at y -1 up without a cap, as the mask keeps all
  above the table) or the bubble's cube, and the free spheres' cubes, clipped at the eye's near plane.
  The rest of the room's or the island's geometry around the table (a large model crossing it is drawn
  whole by the per-object culling) is no longer shaded where the mask would hide it. Logged every 600
  frames: how much of the eye it keeps.
- Each frame's own head pose and mask: aurora::end_frame (the game thread) snapshots the frame's tag
  (aurora_xr_set_frame_info) and diorama mask (xr_present::snapshot_frame); with the async end of
  frame (patch 0018) the GX FIFO worker presents the frame with that snapshot (use_snapshot), as the
  game thread already sets the next frame's meanwhile: the picture was published with the next
  frame's head pose and mask. gfx::end_frame hands the mask to the render worker, which latches it
  (stereo::latch_mask) before the right eye, the masks and the HUD.
- The left eye's passes are encoded as they are recorded, with the mask in effect then (possibly the
  next frame's): their scissors (the window's and the table's) take a margin of 12.5% of the picture a
  side instead of 5%.

